    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q35-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{n-3}{2n}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{n-3}{2n}$.\n\nApply the cosine rule to the angle opposite $n+2$, then expand and factor.",
          "worked": "The longest side is $n+2$, so $\\theta=\\angle ACB$. By the cosine rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{n^2+(n+1)^2-(n+2)^2}{2n(n+1)}\\\\\n\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{n^2-2n-3}{2n(n+1)}\\\\\n\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{(n-3)(n+1)}{2n(n+1)}\\\\\n\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{n-3}{2n}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q35-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that there exists only one such triangle where $\\theta$ is right-angled.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The unique triangle has side lengths $3,4,5$.\n\nSet $\\cos\\theta=0$ to obtain $n=3$.",
          "worked": "If $\\theta=90^\\circ$, then $\\cos\\theta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{n-3}{2n}&=0\\\\\nn&=3.\n\\end{align*}$$\nConversely, $n=3$ gives the valid $3,4,5$ triangle and $\\cos\\theta=0$. Hence this is the only such right-angled triangle.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q35-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that there exists only one such triangle where $\\theta$ is obtuse.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The unique triangle has side lengths $2,3,4$.\n\nThe triangle inequality requires $n>1$, while $\\cos\\theta<0$ requires $n<3$. Thus the only integer possibility is $n=2$.",
          "worked": "For a valid triangle, the two shorter sides must have sum greater than the longest side:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nn+(n+1)&>n+2\\\\\nn&>1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nAn obtuse angle has negative cosine. Since $n>0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{n-3}{2n}&<0\\\\\nn&<3.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe only integer satisfying $1<n<3$ is $n=2$. This gives the valid triangle with sides $2,3,4$ and $\\cos\\theta=-\\frac14<0$. Hence it is the only such obtuse triangle.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q35-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Describe what happens to $\\theta$ as $n$ gets larger. Does this make sense intuitively?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta$ decreases towards $60^\\circ$. The side lengths become nearly equal relative to their size, so the triangle approaches an equilateral shape.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\n\\cos\\theta&=\\frac12-\\frac{3}{2n}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nAs $n$ increases, $\\cos\\theta$ increases towards $\\frac12$, so $\\theta$ decreases towards $60^\\circ$. This makes sense because the fixed differences between $n$, $n+1$ and $n+2$ become negligible relative to their lengths. The triangle therefore approaches an equilateral shape.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
